fortnight is currently one of the most  played game since it hopped on the  recent Battle Royale wave and while it's  available now and free to play on Xbox  one and ps4 the PC version is arguably  the best way to play this increasingly  popular survival shooter while we're  here to break down what kind of visual  quality and frame rates you should  expect with a quick graphics settings  guide with some performance tips this is  an epic games title for night uses  Unreal Engine 4 but when looking at the  minimum specs you'll see the game sets a  very low bar to clear especially by  today's standards at the bare minimum  Epic says you'll need an Intel Core i3  dual-core processor clocked at 2.4  gigahertz 4 gigabytes of RAM and intel  HD 4000 integrated graphics however it's  recommended that you have at least a  core i5 quad-core CPU at 2.8 gigahertz  eight gigabytes of RAM and a gtx 660 or  radeon HD 7870 video card of course  you'd want a little more juice than that  to get an ideal experience with this  game so as we did with our graphics  guide feature from player unknowns  battlegrounds we tested out what we  could get out of a modest older  mid-range system our test bench is  equipped with a core i5 35 70 K CPU at  3.4 gigahertz with 8 gigabytes of RAM  and a 4 gigabyte MSI GTX 970 now that  we've outlined our test bench let's peer  into the graphics settings fortnight  allows you to tinker with 4 PC gaming  veterans the graphics settings menu  shouldn't be surprising however with  fortnight's exploding popularity and  free-to-play model there's sure to be  some players wondering what some of  these terms mean and how it effects  their game resolution is the first thing  to address and we recommend using your  monitors native resolution throughout  this video we're using 1920 by 1080 also  known as 1080p we have the game set to  full screen with an uncapped framerate  3d resolution is essentially resolution  scale this allows you to effectively run  a lower in-game resolution without  having to force your computer to change  from its native res it's really only for  low-end systems so you'll definitely  want to keep this out a hundred percent  otherwise your game look nasty view  distance dictates the point at which  minor objects are rendered since this  game revolves around a large scale  battlefield it's best to keep this as  high as possible and here we're able to  run it at the  exciting but it doesn't impact  performance or visual quality that much  you're still able to see structures  buildings and enemies off in the  distance even at lower quality shadows  of determines the quality of object and  player shadows it can also be turned off  completely but we recommend at least  using medium because in a few instances  seeing a player shadow around the corner  or poking out behind cover will help you  track and anticipate enemy movement it  could help you get the jump on them  anti-aliasing gets rid of jaggies around  the edges of objects and surfaces to  higher the setting the smoother and  cleaner the overall image will look the  medium option uses fxaa which doesn't  look the best but hardly affects  framerate but here we have enough power  to spare so we use the high setting  which uses temporal anti-aliasing and  looks real nice textures affects the  detail on surfaces simple enough so long  as your graphics card has enough vram  our 4 gigabyte GTX 970 handles the high  setting without a problem if X is a  little bit tricky and that you won't see  a difference in visual quality and frame  rate until explosions and destruction  fill up the screen since these  situations are the most hectic and most  important to maintain a high frame rate  we're gonna go with a modest medium  setting here post-processing encompasses  effects like ambient occlusion and  dynamic lighting we kept it to medium  and saw some visual improvements but  it's one of the lesser noticeable  settings when it comes to graphical  quality and framerate motion blur comes  down to preference and vsync should  always be left off alright we have our  specs outline and graphics options  explained now we can get into some  performance numbers we're gonna give a  general range of frames per second  throughout the several matches we played  a fortnight battle royale our goal was  to find a balance between frame rate and  visual quality with the settings we  chose and we were able to maintain  between a hundred to 150 FPS as we  traverse the map and comb through the  streets of the more busy city centers  when things got heated in a firefight or  FPS range between 80 to 110 FPS overall  we never experienced the dip below the  gold standard of 60fps say you're on a  slightly lower spec machine we would  recommend turning down anti-aliasing a  notch or two and if you're still in need  of some more frames turning shadows down  will have the biggest impact  turning effects down to low can also  help stability during intense battles we  also tried running the game with  everything cranked up to epic and got an  FPS range of 50 to 85 this isn't bad by  any means so if you want a bit more flow  a mid-range system can handle it on the  flip side with all the eye candy turned  off we were able to get up to 250 FPS  with dips to about 170 FPS during action  this shows a lot of flexibility for  low-end systems to work with fortnight  it's just as we expected foreign aid is  hardly a graphically demanding game it's  no surprise given the art style that's  not to say the game doesn't look great  because there's certainly an appeal to  the more lively animated look a  fortnight however what we atleast know  is that the game runs smoothly with very  few if any technical hiccups despite its  large scale long story short it's  optimized well we hope you learned a  little something about graphics settings  or got some context as to how fortnight  battery outperforms on PC you can also  check out the full written feature on  GameSpot thanks for watching and we'll  see you next time  [Music]
 

